# stack/autoinstall/packages.optional.list — BAKED INTO THE IMAGE, NEVER INSTALLED.
#
# THE ONE THING TO UNDERSTAND ABOUT THIS FILE: it is the opposite of
# packages.list. Everything in packages.list is downloaded AND installed by
# late-command 3c. Everything HERE is downloaded into the same offline repo and
# then left alone, so it is sitting on the box the moment someone opts in — with
# no network, no archive, no CDN.
#
# WHY THE SPLIT EXISTS. SN-012 rules that the graphical layer is installed only
# when the Owner opts in, so these names cannot go in packages.list — that file
# IS the install list. But the opt-in then needed the internet at the exact
# moment it ran, because `apt-get install xrdp` had nothing local to resolve
# against. Measured on the live hub 2026-08-26: the baked repo persists at
# /opt/homehub/apt (238 debs, 183 MB) and carries none of these, and it was not
# even registered as an apt source, so it was inert weight after install day.
#
# That is open-items E1(ii), which named this exact fix: bake them WITHOUT
# installing them, and retain the repo as an apt source on the box. Both halves
# are needed — baking alone leaves a repo nothing can see.
#
# So the opt-in is now offline-capable and the posture is unchanged: nothing
# here is installed until setup-remote-ui.sh is run on purpose.
#
# THE COST IS IMAGE SIZE, and that is the whole trade. These pull a minimal X
# stack; expect the payload to grow by roughly 150-250 MB. If that ever matters
# more than an offline opt-in, delete this file — export-apt.sh treats it as
# optional and bakes only packages.list when it is absent.
#
# FORMAT: identical to packages.list — one name per line, `#` to end of line,
# blanks ignored. The same parser reads both (read_packages_list).
#
# REBUILD THE BAKED REPO WHEN YOU CHANGE THIS FILE, exactly as for packages.list:
#   bash vmtest/export-apt.sh --target hub --out .out/apt --src-iso <ubuntu iso>
# A name added here without a re-export is a name the offline opt-in cannot
# resolve — and it will fail on the box, not in the build.

# ── the SR-015 opt-in remote UI (SN-012) ────────────────────────────────────
# This set is NOT retyped from anywhere: it is exactly what
# stack/remote-ui/setup-remote-ui.sh apt-installs, and the two must agree or the
# opt-in reaches for the network for whatever is missing. vmtest has a check
# that diffs them, so drift is a failed build rather than a failed opt-in.
#
# --no-install-recommends is what the opt-in uses, and export-apt.sh resolves
# with the same flag, so the closure baked here is the closure installed there.
xrdp                  # the RDP server itself
xorgxrdp              # the Xorg driver xrdp drives; without it sessions are black
dbus-x11              # dbus-launch — an XFCE session will not come up without it

# A MINIMAL XFCE, NOT A DESKTOP. Deliberately not xubuntu-desktop or
# xfce4 (the meta-package): the session exists to run one vendor GUI, so it gets
# a window manager, a panel, a terminal and a file manager and nothing else.
xfce4-session         # the session manager ~/.xsession starts
xfwm4                 # window manager
xfce4-panel           # panel
xfce4-terminal        # a terminal inside the session
thunar                # file manager

# The AppImage FUSE shim. Ubuntu 24.04 ships libfuse3; the IceDrive client (the
# first and so far only reason this layer exists) is an AppImage built against
# FUSE 2 and exits immediately without it, with a message about libfuse.so.2
# that reads like a corrupt download.
libfuse2t64
